            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Fits a formula you write to a set of points: it finds the values of the formula's parameters that make it pass as close as possible to the data, and gives them, the fitted curve and how far off it is.\n\nnp.polyfit fits only polynomials. curve_fit fits whatever describes the process: a decay that settles (a exp(-b x) + c), a rise that levels off, a damped oscillation (a exp(-b x) sin(c x + d)), a power law (a x ^ b). The parameters then mean something - how fast a movement settles, the level it settles to, how springy it is.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.curve_fit <formula>\nDefault: a * exp(-b * x) + c.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nscipy.curve_fit m * x + k\nscipy.curve_fit a * x ^ b\n\nTHE FORMULA:\nWritten in x, with any other names as the parameters to find (in the order they first appear). It can use + - * / and ^ (or **) for powers, and exp, log, log10, log2, sqrt, sin, cos, tan, arctan, arcsin, arccos, tanh, sinh, cosh, abs, sign, pi and e.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ny:\nThe data's values. Every input is fitted and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nx:\nWhere the values are, held. Without x they are at 0, 1, 2 ...\n\nguess:\nStarting values for the parameters, held, in the same order. Without a guess every parameter starts at 1. A formula with a sharp bend may need a sensible guess to find the right fit; if none is found, nothing is sent.\n\nformula (text box, also an inlet):\nThe formula, as above.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nparameters:\nThe fitted values, in the order of names.\n\nnames:\nThe parameters' names, in that order.\n\nfitted:\nThe formula's values at each x, with the fitted parameters.\n\nerror:\nHow far off, on average: the root mean square of the misses. Sent first, then fitted, names, parameters.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nNine measurements of something settling - 3.48 down to about 0.5, at x 0 to 4 in steps of 0.5 - with a little noise. The fit gives a about 3, b about 1.43 and c about 0.47: it starts 3 above its resting level of 0.47 and settles at a rate of 1.43. The fitted curve (right) passes smoothly through the noisy points (left). A metro re-sends the data twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.polyfit fits polynomials quickly and always. np.linalg.lstsq fits any sum of functions. scipy.cubic_spline passes exactly through every point.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
